@node intro "iNTRoDuCTioN"

                              iNTRoDuCTioN
                              ~~~~~~~~~~~~
Well after releasing V1.0 it's seems that nobody use S-Watch anymore. It's 
nice to see so many boards using my Clock, so I decide to write an update.
In this new version you could choose between three different Charsets for 
the clock and all colors could be changed. Hope you like it...:)

If you have any suggestions, bugreports, hints & tips for this or other tools
coming from @{"TRSi(/X-iNNoVaTioN)" link trsimember}, feel free to contact us and we will change
it to your needs.

@endnode

@node installation "iNSTaLLaTioN oF WoRLD-CLoCK V1.1"

                       ReQuiReMeNTS & iNSTaLLaTioN
                       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Of course you need any Amigamodel (500-4000T) with Kickstart V37++ and a full
installed and working Amiex (Should work under /X V2.xx, too).

Put WoRLD-CLoCK V1.1 and the icon wherever you want (Make sure that the icon
is in the same drawer like WoRLD-CLoCK, otherwise the tool won't work!).

Copy the icon `T.info' to your BBSCMD/ drawer and change the Location to the
path where WoRLD-CLoCK V1.1 could be found. That's all. To configure the Clock
to your needs take a look to the @{"Tooltypes" link tooltypes}.

@endnode

@node tooltypes "aLL TooLTYPeS uSeD BY WoRLD-CLoCK V1.1"

                                TooLTYPeS
                                ~~~~~~~~~

 LOCATION=<string>       = Here you could enter the Location of your city,
                           i.e. Berlin-Germany. If you don't want to enter
                           a Location, put this Tooltype in Branches. The
                           Clock writes then as Location the name of your BBS.
                           
 GMT=<GMT-Time>          = Here you must define your GMT-Time (GMT=Greenwich-
                           MeanTime). For Germany, it's +1. If you don't define
                           this Tooltype, the WoRLD-CLoCK wouldn't be shown,
                           you only see the time from your board.

 LINE_ART=<char>         = If you want to seperate the Clock from the cities,
                           you could enter here *one* char (i.e. -, +, =) and
                           this char will be used to create two lines between
                           the clock and the cities. If you don't want to 
                           seperate it, put this Tooltype in branches or don't 
                           define a char for it.

 CHARSET=<number>        = I've included three different styles for the clock.
                           Here you could choose which one will be used to
                           show the time. Only 0,1 and 2 are allowed as valid
                           numbers. The numbers are defined as follow:

                           0 = Digits from Amblin (Default!) 
                           1 = Digits from Axe
                           2 = Digits from Count Zero
                     
                           To check out the digits, take a look to @{"Digits" Link Digits}.

All following ToolTypes are the defines for the colors. Valid colornumbers are
30-37, which represents the ANSI-Colors in /X. I recommend not to use 30, as 
this color is black and you won't see very much if you enter it...:)

 MAIN_COLOR=<color>      = This is the color for the Title and the Lines between
                           the clock and the cities. Default is 33 (yellow).

 CITY_COLOR=<color>      = The color for "TIME SHOWING FROM.." and the cities.
                           Default is 36 (light blue).

 DIGITS_COLOR=<color>    = The color for the digits and the citytimes. Default
                           is 35 (lila).

 DATELINE_COLOR=<color>  = The Color for the Dateline (Sunday, March 27th...).
                           Default is 34 (dark blue).



@endnode

